\par\par \b \uc2 Asset Management: How \uc2 Sam Bankman\uc2 -Fried seduced blue-chip investors\b0\par\par\uc2 Harriet Agnew, Asset Management Editor \par \uc2 1,467 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 November 2022\par 06:00\par \uc2 Financial Times (FT.Com)\par \uc2 FTCOM\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The Financial Times Ltd. All rights reserved. Please do not cut and paste FT articles and redistribute by email or post to the web. \par \par \uc2 Plus, pension fund asset allocation, cryto\u8217\'20\'19s corporate raider-in-chief, and an Alex Katz retrospective at the \uc2 Guggenheim\uc2  in New York\par \par \uc2 One scoop to start: Galois Capital, a San Francisco-based hedge fund whose founder is credited with spotting the collapse of cryptocurrency luna earlier this year, has been caught off-guard after about half its assets were left trapped on crypto exchange FTX, which filed for bankruptcy on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Follow the \u8216\'20\'18smart\u8217\'20\'19 money?\par \par \uc2 The demise of FTX marks a stunning collapse for \uc2 Sam Bankman\uc2 -Fried\u8217\'20\'19s crypto empire that was valued at $32bn just months ago.\par \par \uc2 Since its launch in 2019, FTX has raised $1.8bn from a list of shareholders that reads like a who\u8217\'20\'19s who of the investor world. It features Sequoia Capital, \uc2 Thoma Bravo\uc2 , \uc2 BlackRock\uc2 , SoftBank, Singapore state-owned fund \uc2 Temasek\uc2 , Tiger Global and the \uc2 Ontario Teachers\u8217\'20\'19 Pension Plan\uc2 , as well as some of the world\u8217\'20\'19s most-respected hedge fund managers, including \uc2 Brevan Howard Asset Management\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Alan Howard, \uc2 Millennium Management\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Izzy Englander and the family of \uc2 Paul Tudor Jones\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Their collective support helped lend Bankman-Fried\u8217\'20\'19s business empire credibility before its sudden collapse, driven by concerns about its links with his Alameda Research proprietary trading group.\par \par \uc2 Now these investors will be left facing tough questions from their own clients about how they got it so wrong, why they didn\u8217\'20\'19t demand a seat on FTX\u8217\'20\'19s board, and whether they ever really understood how the business was making money.\par \par \uc2 Read the full story here of how Bankman-Fried seduced blue-chip investors, including the surprise phone call from an old university professor that launched private equity investor Orlando Bravo into becoming one of his most prominent and vocal supporters.\par \par \uc2 Pension funds rethink asset allocation\par \par \uc2 When UK pension funds started to buckle under the turbulence caused by the Truss government\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cmini\u8221\'20\'1D Budget in September, senior executives at \uc2 J Sainsbury\uc2  were taking no chances.\par \par \uc2 The supermarket group\u8217\'20\'19s pension fund, which has more than 70,000 members, had weathered the initial market \b \highlight22\uc2 volatility\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But the company, fearful of more ructions once the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  stabilisation measures were withdrawn, hurriedly set up a loan facility for \u163\'00\'A3500mn.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe decided to put a short-term loan in place should [the pension fund] require it,\u8221\'20\'1D said Sainsbury\u8217\'20\'19s chief financial officer \uc2 Kevin O\u8217\'20\'19Byrne\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CIf there was a spike [in gilt yields]\u8201\'20\'09.\u8201\'20\'09.\u8201\'20\'09.\u8201\'20\'09we didn\u8217\'20\'19t want them to have to do anything irrational like selling assets at the wrong time.\u8221\'20\'1D\u160\'00\'A0\par \par \uc2 In the end, the trustees did not need to draw on the cash. Shortly after the loan facility was set up, Britain changed chancellor and the markets stabilised.\par \par \uc2 But the episode illustrates how wild swings in the gilt markets have taught UK pension funds an important lesson: assets that cannot be quickly converted to cash may deliver higher returns, but in a crisis pension schemes need assets they can sell fast.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19ll be a new focus on liquidity,\u8221\'20\'1D says Andrew Lewis, chief financial officer of FTSE 250 aerospace and defence group \uc2 Chemring\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t think people really understood the liquidity risk, which is very different from the funding risk.\u8221\'20\'1D\u160\'00\'A0\par \par \uc2 The immediate impact of the gilt market chaos has been to shine a light on liability-driven investing, which has become a widely used strategy over the past two decades among the UK\u8217\'20\'19s 5,200 defined benefit (DB) pension schemes.\par \par \uc2 But, as we explore in this Big Read, as the dust settles, there are likely to be important reverberations throughout the rest of the investment sector. The market chaos is accelerating the rethink that was already under way among pension funds about the so-called 60/40 allocation of assets between equities and bonds, which has performed poorly during this year\u8217\'20\'19s sell-off.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s been \u8220\'20\'1Ca nightmare for allocation,\u8221\'20\'1D says \uc2 Vincent Mortier\uc2 , chief investment officer at \uc2 Amundi\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CThe 60/40 model has been broken for some time and totally broken for a year.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 And the renewed focus on liquidity could reduce the amount that pension funds seek to allocate to private markets \u8212\'20\'14 despite a government push to get them to invest more in infrastructure projects.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn the aftermath of the LDI crisis the strategic asset allocation of DB schemes will change materially,\u8221\'20\'1D says \uc2 Michael Eakins\uc2 , chief investment officer at \uc2 Phoenix Group\uc2 , one of the UK\u8217\'20\'19s largest savings and retirement businesses. \u8220\'20\'1CThey will be less invested in private markets and more in liquid markets like bonds and gilts.\u8221\'20\'1D\u160\'00\'A0\par \par \uc2 What does the LDI crisis mean for asset allocation? Email me: harriet.agnew@ft.com\par \par \uc2 Chart of the week\par \par \uc2 Binance\uc2  was going to buy FTX after it suffered a liquidity crunch. But then it thought better of it. One of the world\u8217\'20\'19s largest crypto trading venues, \uc2 Binance\uc2  has long left its rival \u8212\'20\'14 which is primarily a derivatives exchange \u8212\'20\'14 trailing in the dust. Don\u8217\'20\'19t miss this profile of crypto\u8217\'20\'19s corporate raider-in chief, \uc2 Binance\uc2  founder Changpeng \u8220\'20\'1CCZ\u8221\'20\'1D Zhao. And here\u8217\'20\'19s the FT Cryptofinance newsletter on the fledgling sector\u8217\'20\'19s Lehman moment.\par \par \uc2 Highlights from the FT Future of Asset Management Europe conference\par \par \uc2 The crisis in the UK pensions market exposed the \u8220\'20\'1Cconflict of interests\u8221\'20\'1D at investment consultants and they ought to be regulated, according to \uc2 Schroders\uc2  chief executive \uc2 Peter Harrison\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Peter Singlehurst, head of private companies at Baillie Gifford, warned fast-growing private companies face a reckoning next summer, and says the \u163\'00\'A3228bn Edinburgh-based asset manager is \u8220\'20\'1Cnot ready to give up on China\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Top fund management executives have voiced concerns that fragmented \b \highlight22\uc2 regulation\b0 \highlight\uc2  will hold them back. \u8220\'20\'1CMy big concern is around the federalisation or fragmentation of \b \highlight22\uc2 regulation\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D said Patrick Thomson, chief executive for Emea at \uc2 JPMorgan Asset Management\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CAdding complexity to fit a local narrative might not be the best outcome for customers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 10 unmissable stories this week\par \par \uc2 Bond fund managers struggling through one of their worst years in decades say the tide is turning as they position to woo investors attracted by higher yields. \u8220\'20\'1CThe fixed income team is now as upbeat as I\u8217\'20\'19ve seen them for a long time,\u8221\'20\'1D says Stephanie Butcher, chief investment officer at $1.3tn asset manager Invesco.\par \par \uc2 Baillie Gifford\u8217\'20\'19s Scottish Mortgage Investment Trust, one of the biggest China bulls in recent years, has slashed its exposure there, including longstanding investments in tech giants \uc2 Alibaba\uc2  and Tencent.\par \par \uc2 Millennium Management\uc2 , one of the world\u8217\'20\'19s biggest hedge fund firms, has unveiled a shake-up of the organisation of its investment operations that will see co-chief investment officer Bobby Jain depart.\par \par \uc2 Big-name hedge fund managers, including \uc2 Third Point\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Daniel Loeb\uc2 , \uc2 Elliott Management\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Paul Singer\uc2  and CQS\u8217\'20\'19s Sir \uc2 Michael Hintze\uc2 , are snapping up bargains in junk bonds and other corners of the corporate debt market, as they bet a sell-off sparked by the darkening global economic outlook has gone too far.\par \par \uc2 An important financial backer of billionaire Josh Harris\u8217\'20\'19s new asset management venture has received a rare public rebuke from \uc2 Brookfield Asset Management\uc2 , which complained that AEL, a life insurance company, should not be committing funds to a start-up.\par \par \uc2 Oaktree Capital\uc2  has sold a vast plot of land seized from \uc2 Evergrande\uc2  in Hong Kong that was intended for the construction of a Versailles-inspired mansion, forcing the heavily indebted Chinese property developer to take a $770mn loss on one of its most significant assets. \u160\'00\'A0\par \par \uc2 Losses at \uc2 Tiger Global Management\uc2  continued to mount in October after the New York-based hedge fund was buffeted by the whipsawing value of technology stocks in the US and a sell-off in China. The firm\u8217\'20\'19s flagship hedge fund lost 5.4 per cent in October, taking losses this year to a new low of 54.7 per cent. \u160\'00\'A0\par \par \uc2 Fundraising at \uc2 Carlyle Group\uc2  slowed in the third quarter, leading to a decline in assets under management while the private equity investment group searches for a new chief executive after the abrupt departure of \uc2 Kewsong Lee\uc2  in August.\par \par \uc2 The Investment Association\uc2 , which represents British fund managers, has told company boards to keep pay for chief executives and other senior managers \u8220\'20\'1Cin check\u8221\'20\'1D this year to reflect the pain being inflicted on many of their staff by the cost of living crisis.\par \par \uc2 Investment managers are betting that the special characteristics of the British investment trust \u8212\'20\'14 an investment vehicle structured as a publicly traded company that is rare elsewhere in the world \u8212\'20\'14 can help them weather the storms now hitting the markets.\par \par \uc2 And finally\par \par \uc2 A retrospective of painter Alex Katz at the \uc2 Guggenheim\uc2 , New York focuses on the muse who brought his work into focus. This painting of Katz\u8217\'20\'19s wife \b \highlight22\uc2 Ada\b0 \highlight\uc2  also illustrates the cover of a charming novella, Anita by\u160\'00\'A0Alain Elkann.\par \par \uc2 Document FTCOM00020221114eibe0018i\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 Why is Bitcoin price up today\par \b \uc2 Why is Bitcoin price up today?\b0\par\par\uc2 Cointelegraph By  Kyle White \par \uc2 977 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 November 2022\par \uc2 The Cointelegraph\par \uc2 CONTEL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Cointelegraph 2022. All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Bitcoin, altcoins and stocks rallied after data showed United States payrolls surged, but is the current \b \highlight22\uc2 bullish\b0 \highlight\uc2  momentum sustainable?\par \par \uc2 Bitcoin price is up on Nov. 4, and a market-wide rally in crypto prices suggests that Bitcoin ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://cointelegraph.com/bitcoin-price"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 BTC}}}\uc2  ) , Binance Coin ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://cointelegraph.com/binance-coin-price-index"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 BNB}}}\uc2  ) and Ether ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://cointelegraph.com/ethereum-price"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ETH}}}\uc2  ) are starting the month of November in the black.\par \par \uc2 Bitcoin and the market are fighting back after the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Nov. 2 announcement of a 0.75 basis point interest rate hike which at first had a positive impact on equities and cryptocurrency markets, then led to a market downturn.\par \par \uc2 Despite the downward pressure, Bitcoin maintained the $20,000 price floor and now is over {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://cointelegraph.com/news/bitcoin-bulls-face-21k-sellers-as-btc-price-wipes-out-fed-fomc-losses"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $21,000 as the general market responds}}}\uc2  to strong jobs numbers and employment data.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://s3.cointelegraph.com/uploads/2022-11/0d0d91be-d5a3-4696-a194-8587393668b2.png"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Click to view image.}}}\par \par \uc2 BTC price. Source: Cointelegraph\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/news.release/pdf/empsit.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jobs report}}}\uc2  shows payrolls grew 261,000 in October as labor participation fell. The report sent equities upwards as investors analyzed the numbers to show an overall resiliency in the market even against the expected Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC) raised rates by 0.75%.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://cointelegraph.com/news/research-report-outlines-why-the-crypto-market-might-be-on-the-verge-of-a-reversal"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 As reported by Cointelegraph}}}\uc2 , Bitcoin and other cryptocurrencies like Ether and BNB will likely remain closely correlated to U.S. equities 